First, skip the installers; use their Docker image as a sandbox so nothing clogs your machine.  
`docker run --rm -it continuumio/anaconda3 python3`

Then fire up the auto-grader so you can see exactly what counts as “correct.”  
`git clone https://github.com/automata-bike/python-grader && cd python-grader`

Pick one small problem set—say, “FizzBuzz”—and write the whole thing in a single file.  
```python
def solution(n):
    res = []
    for i in range(1, n + 1):
        if i % 15 == 0: res.append("FizzBuzz")
        elif i % 3 == 0: res.append("Fizz")
        elif i % 5 == 0: res.append("Buzz")
        else: res.append(str(i))
    return res
```

Run the grader against it; if it passes, move to the next one.

While you’re at it, practice reading tracebacks. Python’s error messages are surprisingly polite; they’ll tell you the exact line and the type of exception. Treat each crash like a clue, not a failure.
